<Page title="Methods"><article class="methods"><h1>Sources and methods</h1>
<h2>Which stations count?</h2>
<p>The directory starts with the project's public station roster (Radio and joint Radio/TV recipients), not all FCC facilities. We match licensed, active educational FM facilities using the roster call sign and a filing link or community state. A reviewed roster-to-facility link with public-operator evidence can also qualify: FCC facility classifications do not always describe the operator. Those records link their evidence on the station page. We require a single active, granted primary license contour linked to the facility's license. Construction permits, backups, commercial operators, and unresolved alternatives do not contribute. This filing-based screen is not a live on-air-status check.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.fcc.gov/media/radio/fm-service-contour-data-points">FCC contours</a> predict regulatory service areas, not reception at a particular home. AM and TV coverage are outside this source. Owner profiles use documented parents and combine eligible member polygons before counting. If a documented member lacks a usable footprint, no complete owner estimate is reported.</p>
<h2>2020 residents</h2>
<p>We count April 1, 2020 residents whose Census block internal point lies inside or on the footprint. The internal point is a geographic proxy, not an address. A contour that splits a populated block may include too many or too few residents. The 2020 enumeration is also subject to disclosure protection and coverage error.</p>
<p>Population, coordinates and occupied-unit counts come from the <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial-census/about/rdo/summary-files.html">2020 Census redistricting files</a>. The same Census block population baseline can be found in the counts-bearing TIGER blocks; the occupied-unit table is needed for household weights. We process all 50 states, DC and Puerto Rico, including areas across state lines. We do not extrapolate across international borders or into territories without comparable ACS data.</p>
<h2>ACS estimates</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs/data/summary-file.2024.html">2020–2024 ACS 5-year Detailed Tables</a> describe residents and households over 60 months. Each 2020 internal point is placed into its actual <a href="https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/TIGER2024/BG/">2024 block-group polygon</a>, accommodating changes to identifiers and boundaries. Whole groups count fully; cut groups contribute in proportion to their covered 2020 residents for person measures, or covered occupied units for household measures. Boundary-only contact contributes nothing. These are not block-level ACS observations.</p>
<p>Within each source area this assumes a subgroup is distributed like its weighting population. New development or concentrated populations can violate that assumption. Unavailable cells and partial groups with no usable support and a nonzero estimate are flagged rather than treated as zero.</p>
<p>The 90% margins use the root sum of squared weighted source margins, including the Census approximation for combined cells. They assume negligible covariance. They do not include uncertainty in the contour, allocation method, ownership, or time mismatch. An unavailable margin is not a margin of zero.</p>
<p>Tables: B01003 (population), B01001 (age), B03002 (race and Hispanic origin), B15003 (education), C17002 (poverty ratio), B19001 (household income brackets), B25003 (tenure). Each result identifies its universe. Categories from different tables must not be added together.</p>
